  "id": "Compound::DB08896 - Gene::5243",
  "verbalization": "Regorafenib is implicated in the regulation of Abcb1 through multiple indirect pathways, primarily through its interaction with Cytochrome P450 2c9 (CYP2C9) and its inclusion in a class of inhibitors that target this enzyme. The evidence suggests that Regorafenib may modulate CYP2C9 activity, which in turn influences the binding of Fluconazole or Ivacaftor to Abcb1. Both Fluconazole and Ivacaftor are known to interact with Abcb1, and their binding is dependent on the activity of CYP2C9. This implies that Regorafenib could indirectly affect Abcb1 function by altering the metabolic profile of these substrates. The shared involvement of Regorafenib in CYP2C9 inhibition and its binding to Abcb1 highlights a potential mechanism by which Regorafenib influences drug transport across cellular membranes, possibly through the modulation of transporter activity. The presence of a common ancestor in the pathways suggests a shared regulatory mechanism, reinforcing the idea that Regorafenib's interaction with Abcb1 is mediated through a broader metabolic and transport-related pathway.",
